Coding style\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl1 \v Coding style}
{\xe \v Coding style}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVWR}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVWR}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
The script program is easy and simple.{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
The script program is not case-sensitive: the system converts the script code to lowercase as soon as you run it. The only text that is not converted to lowercase is text inside quotation marks.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
All variables are global. But variables inside a function is local. You cannot reach variables inside a function. Functions cannot reach global variables either. You need to send it as an argument.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
GPIO definitions are global.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
No need to specify a variable type. String, Int or float are the three supported types. When the variable initializes, the system determines if the variable is a string, int or float. Reassigning variable changes the variables nature to the new type\par
 \par
If you use the same variable name with another type initializer, the system converts the type according to the new type, whether you change a variable from text to a number or vice versa.\par
 Maximum allowed variables are 32 (defined in {\field {\*\fldinst { HYPERLINK  \\l "AAAAAAABRT" }{}}{\fldrslt {\cs37\ul\cf2 tokens.h}}}). Space has no effect between words. \par
New line, semicolon, carriage return or NULL terminator determines the end of an instruction line. You can write several instructions inside one line separated by semicolon. For example: \par
 put PA_4, 0; \~ \~delay 4,msec; \~\~ put PA_4,1;\par
 A space separates an instruction from its arguments. For example: arg1,arg2. No space is required between arguments. A comma separates different operands or arguments. Commas should also be used in a for loop, not semicolons. \par}
\par
}}
