\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Aget(Analog Get)\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v Aget(Analog Get)}
{\xe \v Aget(Analog Get)}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVWI}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVWI}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This instruction converts the voltage found on the analog input pin. The value is saved in the given variable.\par
 {\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  {\b  aget GPIO_NAME, VARIABLE_NAME} \par
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b GPIO_NAME:}  Make sure that you choose a GPIO that supports analog input. Otherwise, the system will exit and stop executing the code. Since this is specific to the board or CPU used, it is not possible to specify here which pins or GPIOs you should use. Please read the manual for your board.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b VARIABLE_NAME:}  The instruction requires a variable to save the data. The results will be saved in that variable and it is float number.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b VARIABLE_NAME:}  The instruction requires a variable to save the data. The result is saved in that variable as a floating-point value.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b NOTE:}  The value in the variable is a percentage of the real value read by the microcontroller. The value should be multiplied by VCC to get the real voltage. VCC must be stable and free of ripples.\par}
\par
{\i {\b  Example:}  }  \par
 aget PA_4, myvalue; \~\~\~  rem read the value from PA_4 \par
 print myvalue; \~\~\~  rem send the value to terminal  \par
 \par
}}
\par \pard\plain 
\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
APut (Analog put)\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v APut (Analog put)}
{\xe \v APut (Analog put)}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVWJ}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVWJ}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This instruction sends the specified voltage to the analog port using a floating-point value.\par
 {\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  {\b  aput GPIO_NAME, VARIABLE_NAME/Number =[0x0 to 0xFFFF] } \par
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b GPIO_NAME:}  Make sure that you choose a port that supports analog output. Otherwise, the system will exit and stop executing the code. Since this is specific to the board or CPU used, it is not possible to specify here which pins or GPIOs you should use. Please read the manual for your board.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b VARIABLE_NAME:}  The variable must be initialized before this instruction or it will fail.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b Float Number:}  Any floating-point number you specify. Please refer to the manual for the analog output.\par
}
\par
{\i {\b  Example:}  }  \par
 myvalue=0.01; \~\~\~  rem Initialize myvalue with 0.01 \par
 aput PA_4, myvalue; \~\~\~  rem send the value to Analog Output port PA_4 \par
}}
