{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This instruction creates a bus or register for input/output GPIOs. You can combine between 2 and 16 GPIOs. The internal definition is a bus, and it is bidirectional. You can change it from an input to an output port without problems.\par
 {\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  \par
{\b  create_reg REG_NAME, GPIO1,GPIO2,....GPIO16 } \par
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b REG_NAME:}  Any name you choose for the register.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b GPIO1,GPIO2,....GPIO16:}  Any digital input or output GPIO.\par}
\par
{\i {\b  Note: }  }  \par
 You cannot create a register for an analog GPIO. This instruction works only for digital GPIOs. The register name is saved globally. Changing the direction of the port (input or output) is supported. {\i {\b  \par
 Example: }  }  \par
 create_reg rg, PB_0, PB_7, pb_14; REM LED1,2,3 myvalue=0b111; \~\~\~  rem Initialize myvalue with 0b111; \par
 put rg, myvalue; \~\~\~  rem send the value to all three LEDs;  \par
}}
