\par \pard\plain 
\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
For loop\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v For loop}
{\xe \v For loop}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVXA}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVXA}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
A for loop is a repetition control structure that allows you to efficiently write a loop that needs to execute a specific number of times. Only two nested for loops are allowed \par
 {\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  {\b  for(init, condition, increment/decrement)} \par
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b init:}  You must provide a variable for the loop and initialize it; for example, myvar=0.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b condition:}  The condition could be one of the following:{
\par
\pard\plain \s121\fi-360\li720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
comparison between the first variable and a value.\par
\pard\plain \s121\fi-360\li720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
comparison between the first variable and another variable.\par
\pard\plain \s121\fi-360\li720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
comparison symbols allowed (<,>,<=, >=, !=, ==).\par}
}
\par
 {\i {\b  Example:}  }  \par
 Ex1:\par
\~ for(i=0,i<20,i++) \{print i;\}; \~\~\~  rem print to the terminal i-values \par
 EX2:\par
 \~ for(i=100,i>0,i\'96) \{print i;\}; \~\~\~  rem print to the terminal i-values  \par
 EX3:\par
\~ myVar=20;\par
 \~ for(i=100,i>myVar,i\'96) \{print i;\}; \~\~\~  rem print to the terminal i-values \par
 \par
 {\b Note:} {
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
You must use Curly Brackets '\{...\}' after for-loop statement.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
More than two nested for loops are not allowed. However, you can combine a while loop with a for loop to achieve the required iterations.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
You must initialize the variable used inside the for loop instruction. Missing that will cause run-error.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
Comma is used inside for instruction, not semicolon as for C/C++ \par}
\par
}}
