\par \pard\plain 
\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Function Definition\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v Function Definition}
{\xe \v Function Definition}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVXC}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVXC}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Generic Function Implementation and declaration. Functions allow the user to structure their programs in segments of codes to perform individual tasks. They don't share the same global variables. If any script program wants to send a value or variable to the function, user must make them as an argument to the function and function call\par
 They do not share the same global variables. If a script program wants to send a value or variable to a function, the user must pass it as an argument in the function call.\par
 \par
Functions must be defined in the beginning of the script program. This will allow the system to save the definitions. Later when they are called using "Call" statement, they are executed. \par
 {\i {\b Some notes:} } \par
 {
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
Global variable cannot be accessed inside functions.  \par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
GPIO and other peripherals can be accessed inside functions, they are visible everywhere as they are shared in the system. \par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
If you want to access (a) global variable(s), send the variable name or the value as an argument to the function. \par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
Execution of a function might be slower than running the code without a function. Global variables are backed up before calling the function. \par}
{\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  {\b  Function function_name(arg1, arg2 ..arg5) \{ ...code ...\}} \par
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b arg1,..arg5:}  Arguments provided to the function. It could be a string, number, a variable of array, a variable of numbers\par}
\par
{\i {\b  Warning: }  }  \par
\~ Function names cannot be longer than 20 bytes. This limitation restricts the size of the number generated by the hash calculator. Keep this limit in mind when writing a script program.\par
{\i {\b  Example: }  }  \par
function myFirstF(delay_between_pulses)\{\par
 \~ put PA_4,1;\par
 \~ delay delay_between_pulses,mSec;\par
 \~ put_PA_4,0\par
 \~ delay delay_between_pulses,mSec;\par
 \} \par
 .....SCRIPT PROGRAM ...\par
 ..\par
 ..\par
 ..\par
 \par
 myvariable=10; \par
 while(1)\{\par
 \~call myFirstF(myvariable);\par
 \} \par
{\i {\b } } \par
{\i {\b } } \par
{\i {\b \~} } \par
{\i {\b  {\pard\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brsp20 \adjustright \par}
 } } \par
{\i {\b \~} } \par
{\i {\b } } \par
{\i {\b  Another example: }  }  \par
create_reg myreg PA_10,PB_3,PB_5,PB_4,PB_10,PA_8,PA_9,PC_7\par
 \~\~\~  rem Function definition.\par
 function myfunc()\{ \par
 rem An array variable.\par
 \~snow[]=\{0b11111111,0b11111110,0b11111100,0b11111000,0b11110001,0b11100011,0b11000111,0b10001111,0b00011111,0b00111111,0b011111111\};\par
 \~for(i=0,i<=8,i++)\{\par
 \~\~  rem Loop.\par
 \~\~put myreg, snow[i];\par
 \~\~delay 200,msec\par
 \~\}\par
 \}\par
 \par
 \par
 while(1) \par
 \{\par
 \~ rem call the function.\par
 \~call myfunc();\par
 \}\par
 \par
}}
\par \pard\plain 
\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Generate PWM Signal\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v Generate PWM Signal}
{\xe \v Generate PWM Signal}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVXL}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVXL}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This instruction generates a PWM signal defined by a period and a pulse width.\par
 {\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  {\b  pwm PinName, period, pulswidth } \par
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b period:}  Period is a float number in seconds which represent the time between two consecutive low to high transitions in sec.\par
 Make sure that you choose the correct port name. You find the pin names in the manual/datasheet of your board.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b period:}  The period is a floating-point number in seconds that represents the time between two consecutive low-to-high transitions. Make sure that you choose the correct port name. You can find the pin names in the manual or datasheet for your board.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b Pulse Width:}  The pulse width is a floating-point number in seconds that represents the time the signal remains high.\par}
\par
{\i {\b  Example:}  }  \par
 pwm PA_0,0.002,0.001 \~\~\~  rem square wave generated at pin PA_0 \par
 \par
}}
\par \pard\plain 
\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Get (Digital In)\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v Get (Digital In)}
{\xe \v Get (Digital In)}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVXE}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVXE}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This instruction reads from a digital port the value available on the pin/bus. The internal definition of these GPIO will be DigitalInOut and configured as Input for this instruction.\par
 Bus or Register GPIO needs to be created before you can use them using create_reg instruction. {\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  {\b  get GPIO_NAME/BUS_name, VARIABLE_NAME} \par
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b GPIO_NAME:}  Any digital GPIO\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b BUS_name:}  A combination of multiple digital GPIOs, from 2 to 16 pins. You can combine any GPIO in the bus. Use the create_reg instruction to create a register.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b VARIABLE_NAME:}  A variable name is required to save the read data. The results will be saved in that variable and it is float.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b VARIABLE_NAME:}  A variable name is required to save the read data. The result is saved in that variable as a floating-point value.\par}
\par
{\i {\b  Example:}  }  \par
 get PA_4, myvalue; \~\~\~  rem read the value from PA_4 \par
 print myvalue; \~\~\~  rem send the value to terminal \par
 OR:\par
 create_reg myreg , PA_3,PA_2,PA_10_PB3; \~\~\~  rem create register \par
 get myreg, myvalue; \~\~\~  rem read the Register \par
 print myvalue; \~\~\~  rem send the value to terminal \par
 \par
}}
\par \pard\plain 
\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Goto\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v Goto}
{\xe \v Goto}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVXF}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVXF}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
A goto statement provides an unconditional jump from the 'goto' to a labeled statement or start of a script program. It must be in the same function\par
 Goto might be a better choice than while loop or for loop since there are less calculations in the script engine. This can increase speed and performance of the script program. {\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  {\b  goto LABEL }  Note: Label: must be defined for goto statment works. If you jump to an undefined label, script program execution will fails. {\i {\b  \par
 Example: }  }  \par
 goto exitPoint; \~\~\~  rem goto exit \par
 print "Unexecuted instruction\\n";\par
 ....\par
 :exitPoint: \~\~\~  rem Label definition \par
 print "I am going out!!\\n"; \~\~\~  rem send to serial "I am going out!!" \par
 \par
}}
\par \pard\plain 
\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Help\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v Help}
