Variables\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl2 \v Variables}
{\xe \v Variables}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAVYA}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAVYA}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Variables are used to store information that can be referenced and manipulated in a script program. Variables inside the system are global.\par
 To free memory, use the {\b del}  command. Some names are reserved for instructions command. Look at the instruction commands to know the reserved name list.\par
Addition between array variables is not implemented. An array can be passed to a function using its variable name. The function can read all values represented by the variable if it is an array. Variable names are not case sensitive. This means that for example:\par
 MyVariable is equal to myvariable and they are the same name of one variable. \par
\par
This system doesn't care about the case of the letters used in the script program. They all will be changed to lower case during the initialization and run of the script program. \par
 {\i {\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {\tc\tcl4 Syntax: } \par}
} \par
{\i }  {\b  MyVariable=arithmetic expression; }  \par
OR\par
 {\b  MyVariable[\~]=\{nr1,nr2...etc\}; }  {\i {\b  \par
 Example: }  }  \par
 var1=0; \~\~\~  rem define and initialize var1 to be zero\par
 var2=var1+(5*2)^2;\par
 var2++;\par
 var1=var2/100;\par
 array[\~ ]=\{10,10,10,10,10,1\};\par
 array[4]=9;\par
 neVar= log(10); \par
 delete var2;  rem Free up the memory\par
{\i {\b Note:}  }  \par
 Before using any variable in the right side in any arithmetical operation/calculation, you need to assign a value to the variable before that. Not assigning a value to the variable, means that you are using a variable that doesn't exist in the script. This will cause a fatal error. \par
}}
